A recurrence relation is a mathematical equation that describes a sequence of values, each of which is a function of the preceding values. In the context of algorithmic scrutiny, recurrence relations are used to describe the time complexity of algorithms, particularly those that use recursion.

The time complexity of an algorithm is a measure of the amount of computational time required to run the algorithm. This is often expressed in terms of Big O notation, which describes the upper bound of the time complexity. For example, an algorithm that has a time complexity of O(n^2) would have a time complexity that grows quadratically with the size of the input.

Recurrence relations are used to describe the time complexity of recursive algorithms. A recursive algorithm is an algorithm that calls itself during its execution. The time complexity of a recursive algorithm can often be expressed as a recurrence relation, which describes the time complexity of the algorithm in terms of the time complexity of smaller instances of the algorithm.

For example, consider the recursive algorithm for computing the factorial of a number:

```
function factorial(n) {
  if (n == 0) {
    return 1;
  } else {
    return n * factorial(n-1);
  }
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm can be expressed as a recurrence relation:

T(n) = T(n-1) + O(1)

This recurrence relation describes the time complexity of the algorithm in terms of the time complexity of a smaller instance of the algorithm (T(n-1)).

def merge_sort_descending(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    mid = len(arr) // 2
    left_half = merge_sort_descending(arr[:mid])
    right_half = merge_sort_descending(arr[mid:])
    return merge_descending(left_half, right_half)

def merge_descending(left, right):
    sorted_list = []
    i = j = 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if left[i] > right[j]:
            sorted_list.append(left[i])
            i += 1
        else:
            sorted_list.append(right[j])
            j += 1
    sorted_list.extend(left[i:])
    sorted_list.extend(right[j:])
    return sorted_list

